{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015V1f1E2otowRotkFQxExLk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n4\t§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\n5\tdesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n6\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\n7\ton branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\n8\tdesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\n9\tdraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\n10\tthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n11\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n12\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\n13\tapplies the codex review\n14\t`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n15\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n16\t(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\n17\tcorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\n18\trecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\n19\tvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n20\t(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\n21\ta specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n22\t6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\n23\texhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\n24\treview\n25\t`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n26\t(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\n27\tslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\n28\tmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\n29\twith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\n30\troot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\n31\tand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\n32\tcross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\n33\tand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). **Revision 4\n34\t(2026-10-13, Architect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice\n35\t6b merged (`1db08f7d`: `c3d37586` plus the fixes `a396f996`, `f650e046`,\n36\t`bafdec74`), so that §§2.5-2.6, 5.2, 6.1, 6.3, 7, 8.2 state what\n37\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (`lift-task`, `lower-task`,\n38\t`check-stores!`, `check-pendings!`, `check-dependencies!`,\n39\t`check-custody!`, `discharge!`, `live-pcs`) does; every amended clause\n40\tis marked \"**Amended after 6b**\" with what changed and why, §10.6\n41\tinventories them, and §11 lists the design choices the code made that\n42\tthe owner has not ruled on. Sources: the engineer report\n43\t`collab/1792270000000-engineer-6b-task-transport.report.md` (design bugs\n44\tB1-B4, deviations 1-10) and the three codex reviews\n45\t(`collab/1792280000000-…`, `1792300000000-…`, `1792320000000-…`). The\n46\tfrozen document is not changed by this one. **Revision 5 (2026-10-13,\n47\tArchitect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice 6c merged\n48\t(`781a9ba8`: `47ddb1d9` plus the fix round `dc545aaa`, `30730c51`), so\n49\tthat §§4.1-4.3, 5.2, 5.3, 5.5, 5.6, 6.3, 7, 8.3, 8.5, 9 state what\n50\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc` (`segment`, `space`,\n51\t`phys`, `virt`, `lift-state`, `lower-state`, `canonical-task`,\n52\t`kernel-task`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task`, `lower-task`) and the 6c\n53\ttests (`layout_test.cljc`, the harness's cross-engine lanes,\n54\t`store_write_audit_test.clj`) do; every amended clause is marked\n55\t\"**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**\" with what changed and why, §10.7\n56\tinventories them, and §11 gains the three questions slice 6c left for\n57\tthe owner, one of them answered. Sources: the\n58\tengineer report `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`\n59\t(deviations 1-8, design defects D1-D5), the codex review\n60\t`collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md` (F1-F6,\n61\tD1-D5) and the Architect's rulings\n62\t`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md` (R1-R6), whose\n63\twording is applied verbatim where it says so.\n64\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\n65\tunitarity invariant, and to\n66\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n67\t(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n68\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\n69\tand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n70\t\n71\tEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\n72\ttext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\n73\tdocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n74\t`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\n75\tcited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\n76\tits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n77\t(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\n78\tpayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\n79\tto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n80\t\n81\tContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\n82\tframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\n83\tthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\n84\tlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\n85\tverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\n86\tquestions · §12 portability.\n87\t\n88\t---\n89\t\n90\t## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n91\t\n92\t### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n93\t\n94\t1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n95\t   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n96\t   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n97\t2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n98\t   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n99\t3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n100\t   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n101\t   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n102\t   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n103\t   tree set** is incomplete.\n104\t4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n105\t   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n106\t5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n107\t6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n108\t   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n109\t   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n110\t   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n111\t7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n112\t   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n113\t   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n114\t\n115\t### 0.2 The answer\n116\t\n117\tA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\n118\tmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\n119\tframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n120\t(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\n121\tenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\n122\tA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\n123\tevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\n124\tprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\n125\twindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n126\t`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n127\t`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n128\t`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\n129\tvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\n130\tcode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\n131\tnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\n132\tseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\n133\tregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\n134\tdestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\n135\tis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\n136\ta kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\n137\tthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\n138\tre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\n139\tfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\n140\tframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\n141\tand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n142\t(needs 5a/5b).\n143\t\n144\t---\n145\t\n146\t## 1. The canonical frame\n147\t\n148\t### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n149\t\n150\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n151\t`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n152\t`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\n153\tand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n154\t\n155\t```clojure\n156\tact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\n157\ttail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n158\tret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n159\t```\n160\t\n161\t- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n162\t  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n163\t  §1.5).\n164\t- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n165\t  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n166\t  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n167\t- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n168\t  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n169\t  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n170\t- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n171\t  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n172\t  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n173\t  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n174\t  §1.6).\n175\t- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n176\t  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n177\t- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n178\t  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n179\t  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n180\t  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n181\t- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n182\t  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n183\t  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n184\t  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n185\t  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n186\t\n187\t### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n188\t\n189\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n190\t\n191\t```clojure\n192\t;; act state\n193\t{:yin.k/state :act\n194\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n195\t :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n196\t :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n197\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n198\t :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n199\t            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n200\t :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n201\t\n202\t;; tail state\n203\t{:yin.k/state :tail\n204\t :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n205\t :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n206\t :yin.k/k [ … ]\n207\t :yin.k/pending {…}}\n208\t```\n209\t\n210\tThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\n211\tof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\n212\tharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\n213\ttwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n214\t\n215\t---\n216\t\n217\t## 2. The row carrier\n218\t\n219\tThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n220\t**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\n221\treaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\n222\tspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\n223\tpermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n224\t`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n225\t`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\n226\tsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n227\t\n228\t### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n229\t\n230\t**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\n231\tVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\n232\tgate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\n233\tone. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\n234\tleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\n235\tsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\n236\tThe wire unit is the draft's:\n237\t\n238\t```clojure\n239\t{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n240\t```\n241\t\n242\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n243\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n244\t**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\n245\tengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\n246\treceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n247\t(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n248\t\n249\t### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n250\t\n251\t**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\n252\tcontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\n253\tdigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n254\t`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n255\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\n256\tthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\n257\tare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\n258\tcell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n259\t\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\n260\thandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\n261\tbytes of the key as spelled.\n262\t\n263\t### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n264\t\n265\t**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\n266\tand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n267\t\n268\t| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n269\t|---|---|---|\n270\t| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n271\t| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n272\t| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n273\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n274\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n275\t| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n276\t| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n277\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n278\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n279\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n280\t\n281\t`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\n282\tstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\n283\tmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\n284\tredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n285\t`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n286\t\n287\t### 2.4 Pending rows\n288\t\n289\t**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n290\t`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n291\t:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\n292\tchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\n293\tpending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\n294\tthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\n295\tmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n296\t`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n297\t(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n298\t\n299\t### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n300\t\n301\t**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n302\t\n303\t- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n304\t  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n305\t  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n306\t  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n307\t- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n308\t  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n309\t  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n310\t  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n311\t  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n312\t  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n313\t  amendment §7.3, kept).\n314\t- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n315\t  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n316\t  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n317\t- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n318\t  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n319\t\n320\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n321\t(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n322\t\n323\t- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n324\t  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n325\t  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n326\t  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n327\t  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n328\t  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n329\t  never implemented.\n330\t- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n331\t  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n332\t  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n333\t  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n334\t  :missing-module-store`.\n335\t- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n336\t  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n337\t  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n338\t  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n339\t  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n340\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n341\t  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n342\t- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n343\t  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n344\t  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n345\t\n346\t### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n347\t\n348\t**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n349\t\n350\t```clojure\n351\t[T :task\n352\t   version          ; 3\n353\t   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n354\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n355\t   role             ; :root | :child\n356\t   id-counter       ; N\n357\t   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n358\t   store-cref\n359\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n360\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n361\t   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n362\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n363\t   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n364\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n365\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n366\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n367\t   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n368\t   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n369\t```\n370\t\n371\tNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\n372\tprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\n373\tstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n374\t**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\n375\treachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n376\t10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n377\t:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n378\t7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n379\t\n380\t### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n381\t\n382\t**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\n383\tare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n384\t`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\n385\tkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n386\t`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n387\t`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\n388\tvalidator decides.\n389\t\n390\t---\n391\t\n392\t## 3. The frame validator\n393\t\n394\t### 3.1 The pure input contract\n395\t\n396\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\n397\tbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\n398\treturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n399\t(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n400\t\n401\t```clojure\n402\t(frame/defect frame code context task)\n403\t;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n404\t;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n405\t;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n406\t;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n407\t;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n408\t;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n409\t;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n410\t;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n411\t\n412\t(frame/task-defect task)\n413\t;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n414\t;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n415\t```\n416\t\n417\tNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n418\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n419\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n420\t(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n421\t(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\n422\tdecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\n423\tthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\n424\tkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\n425\tsame function on the same inputs.\n426\t\n427\t### 3.2 The rules\n428\t\n429\tRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\n430\tcarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\n431\tbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n432\t(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n433\t(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n434\t(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\n435\tbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n436\t(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n437\t\n438\t**Structure (every frame).**\n439\t\n440\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n441\t|---|---|---|\n442\t| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n443\t| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n444\t| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n445\t| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n446\t\n447\t**Code (every frame).**\n448\t\n449\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n450\t|---|---|---|\n451\t| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n452\t| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n453\t| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n454\t\n455\t**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n456\t\n457\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n458\t|---|---|---|\n459\t| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n460\t| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n461\t| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n462\t| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n463\t| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n464\t| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n465\t\n466\t**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n467\t\n468\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n469\t|---|---|---|\n470\t| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n471\t\n472\t**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n473\t\n474\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n475\t|---|---|---|\n476\t| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n477\t| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n478\t| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n479\t\n480\t**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n481\t\n482\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n483\t|---|---|---|\n484\t| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n485\t| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n486\t\n487\t**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n488\t\n489\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n490\t|---|---|---|\n491\t| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n492\t\n493\t**Pending and contexts.**\n494\t\n495\t| # | Rule | Kind |\n496\t|---|---|---|\n497\t| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n498\t| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n499\t| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n500\t| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n501\t\n502\tRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\n503\tcannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\n504\tenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\n505\tevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\n506\tstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n507\t\n508\t### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n509\t\n510\t**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\n511\treview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\n512\thashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\n513\tpending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\n514\tcomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\n515\tcontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n516\t`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\n517\tjudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\n518\tis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\n519\tfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n520\t(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\n521\tand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\n522\tover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\n523\tevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n524\t`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\n525\treconstructs.\n526\t\n527\t---\n528\t\n529\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n530\t\n531\t### 4.1 What a layout is\n532\t\n533\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\n534\tstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\n535\tfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\n536\tphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\n537\tframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\n538\tadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\n539\tcanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\n540\tBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n541\t§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n542\t\n543\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), identity under image collision (R1,\n544\treplacing codex's F2 amendment).** A is the code identity even when\n545\tdistinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums\n546\tand must not select a source segment. The de Bruijn kernels' offset\n547\ttables carry only the image checksum and refuse a second attach of a\n548\tknown checksum, so a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold two segments of one\n549\timage. Until phase 8 removes those kernels, a de Bruijn receiver refuses\n550\ta carried task whose code holds two A of one image before any stream is\n551\tattached (`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image`), and a de Bruijn source\n552\twhose space resolves one image to several A refuses the lift\n553\t(`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`); no enumeration order selects\n554\tan A. The semantic-register kernel keys code by A and holds\n555\talpha-equivalent segments side by side; this limit is the native\n556\tkernels', not the carrier's. *What the code does:* `layout/space`\n557\tindexes segments by A and, as checksums, by `:register-id` and\n558\t`:stack-id`; `image-segment` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n559\t:ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident}` when an identity names more than\n560\tone A and `:unknown-image` when it names none, so a lift that resolves a\n561\tkernel pc, a return pc or a positional closure's body pc through a\n562\tcollided image refuses inside `canonical-task`, before `rows/lift-task`\n563\tserves anything. `lower-defect`'s first clause (`image-collision`)\n564\tderives the receiver's image for every carried vector and refuses\n565\t`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments\n566\t#{A …}}` for the first identity two A share (identities ordered by their\n567\tprinted form), before `rows/lower-task` and before any attach. *Why:*\n568\tcodex F2 showed `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct A and one R\n569\tand one H; editing both kernels' offset tables for a phase-8 deletion\n570\twas not worth a slice. Tested by\n571\t`layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`, including the reference-lane\n572\tsurvivor round trip (laws 1 and 2 over both closures).\n573\t\n574\t**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n575\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n576\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n577\t`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\n578\tthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n579\t`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n580\t`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n581\t`link-format`). **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the public API as\n582\tlanded (engineer deviation 1, codex: acceptable).** The namespace and\n583\tfile are as proposed; the requires are `dao.jing`,\n584\t`yin.vm.debruijn-code`, `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`, the two kernels\n585\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.register`, `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`), `yin.vm`,\n586\t`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.module`, `.analysis`, `.code`, `.derive`,\n587\t`yin.vm.ucf.rows` and `yin.vm.values`; `yin.vm.debruijn` is not\n588\trequired (the chain is read from A's `:closure` owners, not through\n589\t`resolve-name`). The static half is `segment` (one map per A: `:pairs`,\n590\t`:phys`, `:a->h`, `:h->a`, `:layouts`, `:register`, `:stack`, the two\n591\timage ids), `space`, `segment-of`, `register-pairs`, `correspondence`,\n592\t`phys`, `virt`, `live-out`, `register-live`, `stack-layout`, `chain`,\n593\t`env-of`, `frames-of`, `slices`, `offset`, `kernel-kind`. The dynamic\n594\thalf is one dispatching function each way, `lift-state` and\n595\t`lower-state` (contexts from `lift-context` and `lower-context`), with\n596\tthe `act` and `tail` algorithms separate inside; `canonical-value` and\n597\t`positional-value` convert closures and continuations through guest\n598\tcollections. The task level is `canonical-task`, `kernel-task`,\n599\t`attach-space`, `machine-space`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task` and\n600\t`lower-task` (§7). *Why:* one entry per direction keeps the kernel\n601\tdispatch in one place; the algorithms of §4.2-4.3 are unchanged.\n602\t\n603\t### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n604\t\n605\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n606\t(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n607\t`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n608\t:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n609\t`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n610\t`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n611\t\n612\t**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\n613\tv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n614\t`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\n615\tslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\n616\tthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\n617\tis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n618\t`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\n619\tsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\n620\tboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\n621\twith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\n622\tcover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n623\t(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\n624\toperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\n625\tslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\n626\tinverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n627\t`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\n628\tcanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n629\t\n630\t**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n631\t:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n632\t:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n633\t\n634\t1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n635\t   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n636\t   checksum).\n637\t2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n638\t   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n639\t   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n640\t3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n641\t   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n642\t4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n643\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n644\t   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n645\t   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n646\t   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n647\t   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n648\t5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n649\t6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n650\t   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n651\t   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n652\t   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n653\t   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n654\t   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n655\t   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n656\t   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n657\t7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n658\t   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n659\t\n660\t**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n661\t(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n662\t(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\n663\tin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n664\t`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\n665\ttail state carries no window and no `E`).\n666\t\n667\t**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\n668\timage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\n669\tvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n670\t`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n671\t`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\n672\trecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n673\t`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\n674\tfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\n675\trow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n676\t… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n677\t`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\n678\taggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n679\t(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n680\t\n681\t**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n682\t`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n683\t`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n684\t(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n685\t`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\n686\tthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\n687\tthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n688\t`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\n689\tdelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n690\t\n691\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the unique-preimage rule for `virt`\n692\t(codex D3, adopted verbatim by R5).** At a reachable boundary, virt\n693\tfirst considers preimages in live-out*. Exactly one is returned. If\n694\tthere are none, it may return the body's sole named virtual preimage,\n695\tsolely to reconstruct a tampered window for validation. Zero or\n696\tmultiple such preimages, or multiple live preimages, are refused as\n697\t:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout; no enumeration order selects a\n698\tpreimage. The harness checks an ambiguous dead physical slot is\n699\trefused, a unique dead slot yields :window-extra through the frame\n700\tvalidator, and a missing live slot yields :window-missing. *What the\n701\tcode does:* `layout/virt` filters the body's `phys` map for `s` over\n702\tthe sorted `live-out*` set, answers the one live preimage, else the one\n703\tnamed preimage when no live one exists, else refuses with `{:yin.k/pc p\n704\t:yin.k/register s}`. *Why:* the restricted inverse of the paragraph\n705\tabove left a tampered-upward `live` with no reconstruction at all\n706\t(engineer deviation 7), and codex's M9 (take the first of several\n707\tpreimages) survived the suite until `harness/layout-refusals` pinned\n708\tthe ambiguous case over C's reused slots (§8.3 item 12). The fallback\n709\tis a diagnostic extension, not a layout claim, and lives only until\n710\tphase 8.\n711\t\n712\t**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), primitives in the register kernel's\n713\tpayload (codex D4, adopted verbatim by R6).** A primitive function is\n714\tan admitted carrier value (:prim). The current de Bruijn register\n715\tkernel cannot restore a primitive function reachable through its saved\n716\tregister or lexical-frame payload, including nested guest collections,\n717\tclosure captures and reified continuation state. Lowering a task with\n718\tsuch a wait or parked-state payload into that kernel refuses\n719\t:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers, with the payload path,\n720\tbefore stream attachment. This restriction does not prohibit\n721\tprimitives in the task-wide free environment or in carrier values a\n722\tkernel can otherwise hold. Cross-engine acceptance explicitly records\n723\tunsupported payload cases; expanding this receiver domain requires a\n724\tseparate kernel seam. *What the code does:* `lower-defect`'s register\n725\tclause (`state-prim` over every `:wait` row's state, path `[:frames i\n726\t…]`, then every parked-table state, path `[:parked P …]`) finds the\n727\tfirst `:prim` row reachable through a window (`… :window r`), a lexical\n728\tbinding of an `:env` (`… :env sym`, only the names the chain binds at\n729\tthat pc; the free part of `E` is the kernel's `:free-env` and is\n730\texempt), a closure's lexical bindings (`… :env sym …`), a reified\n731\tstate's act, and the elements of `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` and `:map`\n732\trows (index appended). The stack kernel has no such limit and holds\n733\tthe same carriers (`harness/layout-refusals` runs each refused program\n734\tinto the stack lane and checks the reference value). *Why:* the register\n735\tkernel's restore refuses primitives in registers (`vm/machine-data?`,\n736\t`:continuation-registers`); the refusal belongs before attachment and\n737\tin the layout, not in the carrier (engineer deviation 8).\n738\t\n739\t### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n740\t\n741\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\n742\tand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\n743\tpops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n744\t`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n745\t(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\n746\tthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\n747\tcontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\n748\tthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n749\t\n750\t**Static half.**\n751\t\n752\t1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n753\t   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n754\t   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n755\t   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n756\t   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n757\t   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n758\t   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n759\t2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n760\t   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n761\t   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n762\t   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n763\t   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n764\t   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n765\t   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n766\t   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n767\t   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n768\t   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n769\t   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n770\t   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n771\t   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n772\t   syntax).\n773\t\n774\t**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n775\t`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\n776\toperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\n777\tcaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\n778\tslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n779\t\n780\t```\n781\tslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\n782\tslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\n783\tslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n784\t```\n785\t\n786\t(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n787\t0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n788\t\n789\t**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n790\t:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\n791\ta tail `:call`:\n792\t\n793\t1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n794\t   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n795\t2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n796\t   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n797\t3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n798\t4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n799\t   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n800\t   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n801\t   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n802\t   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n803\t   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n804\t5. pending from the engine fields.\n805\t\n806\t**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n807\t`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n808\t`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\n809\tlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n810\t\n811\t**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n812\t`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\n813\tconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n814\t`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n815\t(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n816\t`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6ad7c4d-4e91-4daf-807a-5f71c598506b","uuid":"1484fca4-b294-4de3-b2b2-fdf619bcbefa","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:16:18.763Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\ndesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\non branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\ndesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\ndraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\nthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\napplies the codex review\n`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\ncorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\nrecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\nvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\na specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\nexhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\nreview\n`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\nslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\nmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\nwith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\nroot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\nand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\ncross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\nand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). **Revision 4\n(2026-10-13, Architect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice\n6b merged (`1db08f7d`: `c3d37586` plus the fixes `a396f996`, `f650e046`,\n`bafdec74`), so that §§2.5-2.6, 5.2, 6.1, 6.3, 7, 8.2 state what\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (`lift-task`, `lower-task`,\n`check-stores!`, `check-pendings!`, `check-dependencies!`,\n`check-custody!`, `discharge!`, `live-pcs`) does; every amended clause\nis marked \"**Amended after 6b**\" with what changed and why, §10.6\ninventories them, and §11 lists the design choices the code made that\nthe owner has not ruled on. Sources: the engineer report\n`collab/1792270000000-engineer-6b-task-transport.report.md` (design bugs\nB1-B4, deviations 1-10) and the three codex reviews\n(`collab/1792280000000-…`, `1792300000000-…`, `1792320000000-…`). The\nfrozen document is not changed by this one. **Revision 5 (2026-10-13,\nArchitect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice 6c merged\n(`781a9ba8`: `47ddb1d9` plus the fix round `dc545aaa`, `30730c51`), so\nthat §§4.1-4.3, 5.2, 5.3, 5.5, 5.6, 6.3, 7, 8.3, 8.5, 9 state what\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc` (`segment`, `space`,\n`phys`, `virt`, `lift-state`, `lower-state`, `canonical-task`,\n`kernel-task`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task`, `lower-task`) and the 6c\ntests (`layout_test.cljc`, the harness's cross-engine lanes,\n`store_write_audit_test.clj`) do; every amended clause is marked\n\"**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**\" with what changed and why, §10.7\ninventories them, and §11 gains the three questions slice 6c left for\nthe owner, one of them answered. Sources: the\nengineer report `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`\n(deviations 1-8, design defects D1-D5), the codex review\n`collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md` (F1-F6,\nD1-D5) and the Architect's rulings\n`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md` (R1-R6), whose\nwording is applied verbatim where it says so.\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\nunitarity invariant, and to\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md)\nand [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md).\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\ntext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\ndocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\ncited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\nits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\npayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\nto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n\nContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\nframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\nthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\nlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\nverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\nquestions · §12 portability.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n\n### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n\n1. **A is the only code identity**; new identities digest under Jing's\n   default (BLAKE3), never SHA-256 (`ucf/code-address` is\n   `jing/segment-key`, `ucf.cljc:247-251`).\n2. **No legacy**: one body version, no reader for older bodies, no\n   compat shim, no migration path; clean breaks.\n3. **Unitarity**: the AST is never lost and is always retrievable\n   wherever code is lowered; no design assumes a tree-less receiver; the\n   segment-to-tree association of the phase-5 design (§9.2 there) is a\n   given, including its completeness rule: an absent entry **or an empty\n   tree set** is incomplete.\n4. **The vector-to-datoms mapping is retired**; the carrier carries\n   positional tuples and rows, never datoms-from-vector.\n5. **dao.stream is the complexity boundary**: no transport mechanics.\n6. **One truth, many interpretations**: the register machine's\n   configuration `C = ⟨seg, pc, W⟩` with `E`, `K` is the canonical frame\n   shape; the stack and register kernels reconstruct it through their\n   layouts (frozen §4.3, §10 phase 6).\n7. **No file of phase 4b or phase 5 is touched**, so this phase runs\n   concurrently with them (§8.5 names the dependencies that remain, and\n   the one old-kernel file 6c must change).\n\n### 0.2 The answer\n\nA continuation is a value: one **canonical frame** of the register\nmachine, `act` or `tail`, with its saved window, its `K` of `:return`\nframes, its environment, its delivery record and its pending wait\n(frozen §2.4, §4.2), plus the task context it needs: the task's **free\nenvironment**, store slice, module stores, parked table, cursor cells, code by\nA and the tree association. **§1** restates the frame as the 4b\nevaluator mints it. **§2** gives the row carrier: body version 3, one\nprofile (`\"v4\"`), Merkle content rows under Jing's default digest, the\nwindow as a sorted keyed row in which a nil value is a present\n`[:lit nil]`, `:act`, `:tail`, `:window`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\n`:parked-table` rows replacing the stale stack-shaped rows, a root\n`free-env` slot, the pending rows and census unchanged. **§3** gives the\nvalidator as an ordered set of rules over the decoded frame, the carried\ncode and decoded task context, engine-independent, run after canonical\nnumbering as the carrier orders it. **§4** defines the two layouts with\nseparate `act` and `tail` algorithms: the register kernel's physical\nregisters are paired with A's virtual ones pc by pc and its return\ndestinations recovered from A's call; the stack kernel's operand stack\nis sliced `[previous-base, base_i)` per frame, and its tail completion is\na kernel seam this phase must add and gate. **§5** is the harness over\nthree lanes with an expanded, concrete matrix and a specified\nre-emission procedure for cross-lane equality. **§6** fixes what travels\nfor code and task context. **§8** cuts the slices: 6a the independent\nframe and row codec, 6b full task transport (needs phase 5's scanners\nand 4b stage 2), 6c the layouts and the stack tail-completion seam\n(needs 5a/5b).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The canonical frame\n\n### 1.1 Shapes, as the evaluator mints them\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4 \"Delivery\", §4.2; evaluator design §1.3-1.6;\n`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:61-84` for `act-payload`,\n`entry-builders`, `tail-builders`; `:229-234` for the reified payload\nand the park). Two closed state shapes and one closed delivery record:\n\n```clojure\nact  = {:segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :k K :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd r}}\ntail = {:segment seg :site n :k K :deliver {:deliver :return}}\nret  = {:type :return :segment seg :pc n :env E :window W :rd r}     ; one frame kind of K\n```\n\n- `:pc` of an `act` is the resume pc, already `pc+1` of the parking\n  instruction; `:site` of a `tail` is the tail call's own pc (evaluator\n  §1.5).\n- `W` of an `act` is `saved(pc, rd) = W↾(L(pc) − {rd})`\n  (`analysis/saved`, `analysis.cljc:92-97`), built with `select-keys`,\n  so a nil-valued live register is a present key.\n- `K` is a vector of `ret` frames, innermost last, or nil when empty\n  (`put-registers`, `semantic_register.cljc:47-52`); each frame's window\n  is `saved(frame.pc, frame.rd)` of its own body.\n- A **parked record** (`:park`, and FFI bookkeeping under a call id) is\n  an `act` payload plus `:type :parked-continuation :id`\n  (`engine/park-continuation`; evaluator §1.6); the FFI bookkeeping\n  record of an effectful **tail** call is a `tail` payload (evaluator\n  §1.6).\n- A **reified continuation** is `values/continuation owner (assoc act\n  :type :reified-continuation)` (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:229-232`).\n- The wait entry the engine holds is the `act` or `tail` payload plus\n  the engine's fields (`:reason`, `:cursor-ref`, `:stream-id`, `:datom`,\n  `:call-id`, `:request-sent`, the link fields), which travel as the\n  **pending** (§2.4), never as frame slots.\n- The task's **free environment** is the `:env` a `create-vm` received\n  (`semantic_register.cljc:261`): every activation's `E` is that map\n  merged under the lexical chain's bindings (§4.4), and a de Bruijn\n  kernel holds the same map as its task-wide `:free-env`\n  (`debruijn/stack.cljc:82, 418, 683`; `debruijn/register.cljc:50`).\n\n### 1.2 The UCF frame, keyed form\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2), restated with one naming decision (§10.1 F1):\n\n```clojure\n;; act state\n{:yin.k/state :act\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/pc n\n :yin.k/env   {:yin.k/bindings {sym V …} :yin.k/store-of M?}\n :yin.k/window {r V …}                      ; exactly saved(pc, rd); sorted by r on the wire\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}\n :yin.k/k [{:yin.k/frame-type :return :yin.k/segment A :yin.k/pc n\n            :yin.k/env E :yin.k/window {r V …} :yin.k/rd r} …]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}                        ; absent on a parked record and a reified value\n\n;; tail state\n{:yin.k/state :tail\n :yin.k/segment A  :yin.k/site n\n :yin.k/deliver {:yin.k/deliver :return}\n :yin.k/k [ … ]\n :yin.k/pending {…}}\n```\n\nThis keyed form is the **decoded canonical frame**: what the validator\nof §3 judges, what the layouts of §4 produce and consume, and what the\nharness compares across lanes. The rows of §2 are its carrier and the\ntwo are in bijection (§2.6).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The row carrier\n\nThe row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`) is adopted\n**except** where it describes the stack-shaped machine or legacy\nreaders; §10.3 lists every replaced or deleted clause. Its §3 (rows, id\nspaces, census, the explicit \"version 3 verifies under BLAKE3\npermanently\" rule), §4 (value union), §5.1 (value rows, except\n`:reified`), §5.3 (pending), §5.4 (task context, except\n`:parked-table`), §6 (canonical cursor-cell numbering), §7 (equality) and §8\nsteps 1-6 and 8-12 stand.\n\n### 2.1 Positioning: body version 3, one profile\n\n**DECIDED** by ruling 2 and ruling 6. The carrier is **body version 3**.\nVersions 0-2 are the old handoff's; the row reader refuses them at the\ngate (`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) and never reads, normalises or upgrades\none. Their reader (`yin.vm.ucf.handoff`) is untouched by this phase and\nleaves with the stack-shaped machine at phase 8; that coexistence is\nsequencing, not permission for the row reader to accept a legacy body.\nThe wire unit is the draft's:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true  :yin.k/version 3  :yin.k/root T  :yin.k/rows [[id kind & slots] …]}\n```\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: ACCEPTED). The profile map is\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}` and nothing else; there is\n**no `:yin.k/engine` key**. The frame is the canonical machine's and\nengine-independent by ruling 6; the kernel that executes it is the\nreceiving composition's choice. The version-2 registry\n(`ucf/profiles`, `ucf.cljc:51-74`) is not consulted.\n\n### 2.2 Rows, ids, census\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §3, ruling 1). Every row is `[id kind & slots]`;\ncontent rows have `id = (jing/segment-key body)` under Jing's default\ndigest, BLAKE3 for version 3 permanently; identity rows are cursor cells\n`:yin.k/cc-<n>` numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`); the root carries\nthe cursor-cell census. (The words cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref and cell\nare used exactly as defined in UCF §7.5.3, \"Terminology: cursor, cursor\ncell, cursor-ref, cell\". Before 2026-10-12 the cursor cells were called\n\"cells\" and spelled `:yin.k/c-<n>`, `:cells`, `:cell-body`; the version-2\nhandoff carrier keeps those spellings.) One ordering rule: \"sorted\" is by the Jing canonical\nbytes of the key as spelled.\n\n### 2.3 The frame rows (replacing draft §5.2)\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §4.2's last paragraph; **PROPOSED** names `:act`\nand `:tail` (§10.1 F1, reviewer: ACCEPTED):\n\n| Kind | Slots | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:act` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  k-cref  rd-N` | the delivery record is `{:deliver :rd :rd rd-N}` by kind |\n| `:tail` | `segment-A  site-N  k-cref` | delivery `{:deliver :return}` by kind; **no** env, window or pc slot |\n| `:window` | `r₁ V₁  r₂ V₂  …` sorted by `rᵢ` ascending, unique | `rᵢ` is `N`; **a nil-valued register is present as `rᵢ [:lit nil]`**; the empty window is `[:window]` |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` outermost first | `[:k]` is the empty continuation |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  window-cref  rd-N` | one `:return` frame |\n| `:wait` | `state-cref  pending-cref` | `state-cref` is an `:act` or a `:tail` row |\n| `:reified` | `act-cref` | a captured continuation value; its act's `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation` |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | unchanged |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | unchanged (draft §5.1) |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁  S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | unchanged |\n\n`:window`, `:bindings`, `:store` and every other tabled keyed row are\nstructural rows, never a `V`; the draft's \"a reference-free collection\nmust be `[:lit d]`\" rule is about guest collections. `rd` on `:act` is\nredundant with the instruction at `pc−1` and is checked (rule A2), as\n`pc` is redundant with the pending and checked.\n\n### 2.4 Pending rows\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §5.3, UCF §7.4.3, v2 amendment §2): one kind\n`:pending`; reasons `:next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request\n:link-response :install` with the draft's slots, unchanged. What\nchanges is only which state a pending rides on: a `:wait` pairs a\npending with an `:act` (write-result) **or a `:tail`** (through-return),\nthe frozen §4.1 table's two state columns. The 4b evaluator's in-VM\nmarkers (`:request-sent`, `:call-id`, `:op`, `:datom`,\n`:response-cursor`, `:response-stream`) are the pending's\n(`handoff.cljc:702-724`, `:3219-3229`).\n\n### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n\n- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n  amendment §7.3, kept).\n- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n\n- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n  never implemented.\n- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :missing-module-store`.\n- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\nstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\nreachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n\n**PROPOSED.** `rows->frame` and `frame->rows` (`yin.vm.ucf.rows`, §8.1)\nare inverse on valid row sets and canonical keyed frames: `:act` ↔\n`{:yin.k/state :act …}` with the deliver record synthesised from the\nkind and `rd`; `:tail` ↔ `{:yin.k/state :tail …}`; `:window` ↔\n`:yin.k/window` as a map (`[:lit nil]` ↔ a nil-valued key); `:k` ↔\n`:yin.k/k` in order. `rows->frame` reshapes and decides nothing the\nvalidator decides.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The frame validator\n\n### 3.1 The pure input contract\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.2: membership recomputed from A; representation,\nbody scope, site/pc pairing, destination equality, delivery mode,\nreturn-frame sites, `K` structure, pending compatibility). **PROPOSED**\n(reviewer: publish the complete contract):\n\n```clojure\n(frame/defect frame code context task)\n;; frame   : a decoded canonical frame (§1.2), any shape (the rules judge it)\n;; code    : {A vector}, every vector already code/well-formed? (step 4 of the carrier ran)\n;; context : :wait | :captured | :parked\n;; task    : decoded task context, plain data, no engine:\n;;           {:parked-key P-or-nil                 ; the parked-table key this frame sits under (:parked only)\n;;            :ffi-call-ids #{…}                   ; call ids of the task's live :ffi and :ffi-request pendings\n;;            :free-env {sym V …}}                 ; the root's free environment, decoded\n;; => nil, or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n\n(frame/task-defect task)\n;; the once-per-body rule X1 over the decoded task context (its :free-env);\n;; run before any frame of the body; => nil or {:yin.k/kind k :yin.k/path p …}\n```\n\nNamespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.frame`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc`, requiring only\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `yin.vm.debruijn`\n(`resolve-name`, for the chain of rule E2) and `yin.vm`\n(`reserved-name?`). It knows no kernel and reads no engine: `task` is\ndecoded from the rows (the parked table's key, the pendings' call ids,\nthe root's `free-env`), never from a running machine. A frame a stack\nkernel lifted and one the register evaluator lifted are judged by the\nsame function on the same inputs.\n\n### 3.2 The rules\n\nRules run in the order listed; the first defect wins; every defect\ncarries `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/path`. **Structural rules S1-S4 run\nbefore any dependent read**, so later rules may index freely. `a =\n(analysis/analyze v)` per segment; `body(p) = (code/body-index\n(code/body-ranges v) p)`; `k(body)` the body's register count\n(`code/parse`); `inst(p) = (nth v p)`; `chain(b)` the owner chain of\nbody `b` as phase-5 §1.2 (closure pcs innermost first) and `params(c) =\n(nth (nth v c) 2)`.\n\n**Structure (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | `frame` is a map; `:yin.k/state` is `:act` or `:tail`; an `act` has exactly the keys `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending`; a `tail` exactly `:yin.k/state :yin.k/segment :yin.k/site :yin.k/deliver :yin.k/k` plus optionally `:yin.k/pending` | `:frame-shape` |\n| S2 | `:yin.k/k` is a vector; every element is a map with exactly `:yin.k/frame-type :yin.k/segment :yin.k/pc :yin.k/env :yin.k/window :yin.k/rd`, `:yin.k/frame-type` is `:return` | `:k-shape` (path `[:yin.k/k i]`) |\n| S3 | every `:yin.k/window` present (the act's, each return frame's) is a map whose keys are exact non-negative integers (on the wire: sorted ascending, unique) and whose values are `V` | `:window-representation` |\n| S4 | every `:yin.k/env` present is `{:yin.k/bindings {S V …}}` optionally with `:yin.k/store-of M`; `:yin.k/deliver` is a map | `:env-shape` / `:deliver-shape` |\n\n**Code (every frame).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 | `:yin.k/segment` of the frame and of every return frame names a key of `code` | `:segment` |\n| C2 | an `act`'s `pc`, every return frame's `pc` and a `tail`'s `site` are **exact non-negative integers** (`cbor/numeric-kind` `:integer` for a wire value; never a float carrier), checked **before** any indexing; then `0 < pc < (count v)` and `0 ≤ site < (count v)` | `:pc-kind` / `:pc-bounds` |\n| C3 | `body(pc−1) = body(pc)` for the act and every return frame (no body ends in a parking instruction, frozen §3.4 rule 6) | `:pc-body` |\n\n**The current activation (`act` only; a `tail` skips A1-A6).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | `inst(pc−1)` is one of `:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation`, or a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:site` |\n| A2 | `(:yin.k/rd deliver) = (code/rd inst(pc−1))` and `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :rd :yin.k/rd r}` exactly | `:destination` / `:deliver` |\n| A3 | every window key `< k(body(pc))` | `:window-scope` |\n| A4 | `rd ∉ (keys window)` | `:window-self` |\n| A5 | `(set (keys window)) ⊆ (analysis/saved a pc rd)` | `:window-extra` |\n| A6 | `(analysis/saved a pc rd) ⊆ (set (keys window))` | `:window-missing` |\n\n**The tail site (`tail` only).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | `inst(site)` is a `:call` with `tail?` true; `deliver = {:yin.k/deliver :return}` exactly | `:site` / `:deliver` |\n\n**Return frames (every frame, each `K[i]`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| K1 | `inst(frame.pc−1)` in `frame.segment` is a `:call` with `tail?` false | `:return-site` |\n| K2 | `frame.rd = (code/rd inst(frame.pc−1))` | `:return-destination` |\n| K3 | every key of `frame.window` `< k(body(frame.pc))`; `frame.rd ∉ keys`; `(set (keys frame.window)) = (analysis/saved aₛ frame.pc frame.rd)` in that segment's analysis, reported as `:return-window-scope` / `:return-window-self` / `:return-window-extra` / `:return-window-missing` | as named |\n\n**Environments (every `E`: the act's and each return frame's).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | no binding key is reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`) | `:reserved-name` |\n| E2 | **`E` is the free environment under the lexical chain.** With `C = (set (mapcat params (chain (body pc))))` the lexically bound names at that pc (a return frame's chain is that of its own `pc`): (a) **every name of `C` is a key of `bindings`**, a nil binding being a present key (`bind-params` nil-fills, so a lexical name is never absent; an absent one would be nil-filled on lower and break the canonical round trip); (b) `(apply dissoc bindings C) = (apply dissoc (:free-env task) C)` as maps of `V`: a key outside `C` the free environment lacks, a free key `E` lacks, or a differing value, is a defect | `:free-env-mismatch` |\n\n**Task (once per body, before any frame; `frame/task-defect`).**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| X1 | the root's `free-env` decodes to a map `{S V …}` with symbol keys, sorted and unique on the wire, none reserved (`vm/reserved-name?`, the same check `create-vm` runs on its `:env`, `vm/check-bindings!`, `vm.cljc:161-168`); it is the `:free-env` every E2 compares against | `:free-env-shape` / `:reserved-name` (path `[:free-env]`) |\n\n**Pending and contexts.**\n\n| # | Rule | Kind |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `context :wait` requires `:yin.k/pending` present; `:captured` and `:parked` require it absent | `:pending-presence` |\n| P2 | (`:wait`) the pending's reason is admitted by `inst(pc−1)` (act) or `inst(site)` (tail): `:stream-next → :next`; `:stream-put → :put`; `:ffi-call → :ffi, :ffi-request`; `:call → :next :put :ffi :ffi-request :link-request :link-response :install`; `:park` and `:current-continuation` admit none (frozen §4.1; `wire-of-kind`, `handoff.cljc:491-504`; ucf-revisions §8) | `:reason-mismatch` |\n| P3 | (`:captured`) the frame is an `act` and `inst(pc−1)` is `:current-continuation` | `:captured-site` |\n| P4 | (`:parked`) either `inst(pc−1)` is `:park` (an `act`), or `(:parked-key task) ∈ (:ffi-call-ids task)` and the frame is an `act` with `inst(pc−1) = :ffi-call` or a `tail` whose `inst(site)` is a tail `:call` (bookkeeping, evaluator §1.6) | `:parked-site` |\n\nRule E2 is the correction the review required (§4.4): a merged `E`\ncannot be split into free and lexical parts without the task's free\nenvironment, so the carrier carries it and the validator checks that\nevery `E` agrees with it off the lexical names. Nothing here reads a\nstack depth, a stack base or a `live` vector.\n\n### 3.3 Where and when the validator runs\n\n**DECIDED** (carrier order kept: draft §8, step 7 after step 6; the\nreview's ruling). On **lower**: gate (1), structure (2), graph (3),\nhashes (4), canonical form (5), canonical numbering (6), **frames (7)**,\npending and operations (8), dependency closure (9), custody (10),\ncomposition (11), restore (12). In step 7 the body's decoded task\ncontext is judged first by `frame/task-defect` (X1), then every `:act`,\n`:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and parked-table state is decoded and\njudged by `frame/defect` with its context and that task context; a defect\nis `:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path. A\nfirst-defect test therefore sees a numbering defect\n(`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`) before a frame defect on a body that has both,\nand §8's acceptance items say so. On **lift**: the same function runs\nover every assembled frame before any row is emitted; a frame the\nevaluator itself minted that fails is `:yin.k/non-portable`\n`:inconsistent-state`. The harness runs it over every frame a layout\nreconstructs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n\n### 4.1 What a layout is\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\nstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\nfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\nphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\nframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\nadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\ncanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\nBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), identity under image collision (R1,\nreplacing codex's F2 amendment).** A is the code identity even when\ndistinct A derive identical H or R. Native-image hashes remain checksums\nand must not select a source segment. The de Bruijn kernels' offset\ntables carry only the image checksum and refuse a second attach of a\nknown checksum, so a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold two segments of one\nimage. Until phase 8 removes those kernels, a de Bruijn receiver refuses\na carried task whose code holds two A of one image before any stream is\nattached (`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image`), and a de Bruijn source\nwhose space resolves one image to several A refuses the lift\n(`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image`); no enumeration order selects\nan A. The semantic-register kernel keys code by A and holds\nalpha-equivalent segments side by side; this limit is the native\nkernels', not the carrier's. *What the code does:* `layout/space`\nindexes segments by A and, as checksums, by `:register-id` and\n`:stack-id`; `image-segment` refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n:ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident}` when an identity names more than\none A and `:unknown-image` when it names none, so a lift that resolves a\nkernel pc, a return pc or a positional closure's body pc through a\ncollided image refuses inside `canonical-task`, before `rows/lift-task`\nserves anything. `lower-defect`'s first clause (`image-collision`)\nderives the receiver's image for every carried vector and refuses\n`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments\n#{A …}}` for the first identity two A share (identities ordered by their\nprinted form), before `rows/lower-task` and before any attach. *Why:*\ncodex F2 showed `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct A and one R\nand one H; editing both kernels' offset tables for a phase-8 deletion\nwas not worth a slice. Tested by\n`layout-test/alpha-equivalent-segments`, including the reference-lane\nsurvivor round trip (laws 1 and 2 over both closures).\n\n**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\nthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n`link-format`). **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the public API as\nlanded (engineer deviation 1, codex: acceptable).** The namespace and\nfile are as proposed; the requires are `dao.jing`,\n`yin.vm.debruijn-code`, `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`, the two kernels\n(`yin.vm.debruijn.register`, `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`), `yin.vm`,\n`yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.module`, `.analysis`, `.code`, `.derive`,\n`yin.vm.ucf.rows` and `yin.vm.values`; `yin.vm.debruijn` is not\nrequired (the chain is read from A's `:closure` owners, not through\n`resolve-name`). The static half is `segment` (one map per A: `:pairs`,\n`:phys`, `:a->h`, `:h->a`, `:layouts`, `:register`, `:stack`, the two\nimage ids), `space`, `segment-of`, `register-pairs`, `correspondence`,\n`phys`, `virt`, `live-out`, `register-live`, `stack-layout`, `chain`,\n`env-of`, `frames-of`, `slices`, `offset`, `kernel-kind`. The dynamic\nhalf is one dispatching function each way, `lift-state` and\n`lower-state` (contexts from `lift-context` and `lower-context`), with\nthe `act` and `tail` algorithms separate inside; `canonical-value` and\n`positional-value` convert closures and continuations through guest\ncollections. The task level is `canonical-task`, `kernel-task`,\n`attach-space`, `machine-space`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task` and\n`lower-task` (§7). *Why:* one entry per direction keeps the kernel\ndispatch in one place; the algorithms of §4.2-4.3 are unchanged.\n\n### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n\n**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\nv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\nslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\nthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\nis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\nsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\nboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\nwith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\ncover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\noperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\nslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\ninverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\ncanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n\n1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n   checksum).\n2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\nin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\ntail state carries no window and no `E`).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\nimage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\nvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\nrecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\nfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\nrow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\naggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n\n**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\nthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\nthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\ndelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the unique-preimage rule for `virt`\n(codex D3, adopted verbatim by R5).** At a reachable boundary, virt\nfirst considers preimages in live-out*. Exactly one is returned. If\nthere are none, it may return the body's sole named virtual preimage,\nsolely to reconstruct a tampered window for validation. Zero or\nmultiple such preimages, or multiple live preimages, are refused as\n:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout; no enumeration order selects a\npreimage. The harness checks an ambiguous dead physical slot is\nrefused, a unique dead slot yields :window-extra through the frame\nvalidator, and a missing live slot yields :window-missing. *What the\ncode does:* `layout/virt` filters the body's `phys` map for `s` over\nthe sorted `live-out*` set, answers the one live preimage, else the one\nnamed preimage when no live one exists, else refuses with `{:yin.k/pc p\n:yin.k/register s}`. *Why:* the restricted inverse of the paragraph\nabove left a tampered-upward `live` with no reconstruction at all\n(engineer deviation 7), and codex's M9 (take the first of several\npreimages) survived the suite until `harness/layout-refusals` pinned\nthe ambiguous case over C's reused slots (§8.3 item 12). The fallback\nis a diagnostic extension, not a layout claim, and lives only until\nphase 8.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), primitives in the register kernel's\npayload (codex D4, adopted verbatim by R6).** A primitive function is\nan admitted carrier value (:prim). The current de Bruijn register\nkernel cannot restore a primitive function reachable through its saved\nregister or lexical-frame payload, including nested guest collections,\nclosure captures and reified continuation state. Lowering a task with\nsuch a wait or parked-state payload into that kernel refuses\n:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers, with the payload path,\nbefore stream attachment. This restriction does not prohibit\nprimitives in the task-wide free environment or in carrier values a\nkernel can otherwise hold. Cross-engine acceptance explicitly records\nunsupported payload cases; expanding this receiver domain requires a\nseparate kernel seam. *What the code does:* `lower-defect`'s register\nclause (`state-prim` over every `:wait` row's state, path `[:frames i\n…]`, then every parked-table state, path `[:parked P …]`) finds the\nfirst `:prim` row reachable through a window (`… :window r`), a lexical\nbinding of an `:env` (`… :env sym`, only the names the chain binds at\nthat pc; the free part of `E` is the kernel's `:free-env` and is\nexempt), a closure's lexical bindings (`… :env sym …`), a reified\nstate's act, and the elements of `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` and `:map`\nrows (index appended). The stack kernel has no such limit and holds\nthe same carriers (`harness/layout-refusals` runs each refused program\ninto the stack lane and checks the reference value). *Why:* the register\nkernel's restore refuses primitives in registers (`vm/machine-data?`,\n`:continuation-registers`); the refusal belongs before attachment and\nin the layout, not in the carrier (engineer deviation 8).\n\n### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\nand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\npops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\nthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\ncontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\nthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n\n**Static half.**\n\n1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n   syntax).\n\n**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\noperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\ncaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\nslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n\n```\nslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\nslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\nslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n```\n\n(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\na tail `:call`:\n\n1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n5. pending from the engine fields.\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\nlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\nconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)","numLines":816,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2621,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015V1f1E2otowRotkFQxExLk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
