{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KeCZwuTj9fgrHxTVscq9L1","type":"tool_result","content":"1999\t### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n2000\t\n2001\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 7). 6a-6c green on three hosts; the row\n2002\tcarrier the only writer and reader the semantic-register kernel has; a\n2003\tdocumented seam for the holder, checkpoint, ledger and authority paths\n2004\t(phase 7 wires it; phase 6 edits none of them); the Stage D/E scenarios\n2005\t(`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`) listed with, for\n2006\teach, the §5.2 row that exercises its parking shape; the export-recovery\n2007\tobject (UCF §7.7.4, format version 1 over the old codec) re-based on the\n2008\trow carrier by phase 7, since it embeds a body; and the free-environment\n2009\tretention seam (§6.3) either landed by the 4b track or the explicit\n2010\t`:free-env` argument threaded through the holder's export path.\n2011\t\n2012\t### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n2013\t\n2014\t| | needs | concurrent with |\n2015\t|---|---|---|\n2016\t| 6a | 4b stage 1 (branch) | 4b stage 2, 5a-5c, 6c-pre |\n2017\t| 6c-pre | nothing of 4b or 5 | everything |\n2018\t| 6b | 4b stage 2; 5c (scanners) | 5a, 5b, 6c-pre |\n2019\t| 6c | 5a, 5b, 6b, 6c-pre | 5c |\n2020\t\n2021\tNo file of 4b or phase 5 is edited. The old kernel file\n2022\t`debruijn/stack.cljc` is edited by 6c-pre only, for the seam; the\n2023\tregister kernel is not edited. `handoff.cljc` and the old harness are\n2024\tnot edited. **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13):** 6c proper held to this:\n2025\tit created `layout.cljc` and `layout_test.cljc`, extended the 6b harness\n2026\tfile and the store-write audit's allowlist, and edited no kernel, no\n2027\t`rows.cljc` and no 4b or phase-5 file. The one further kernel edit this\n2028\tdesign names, the retained-FFI response-pair seam of both de Bruijn\n2029\tkernels (§4.3, 6c-post-D2), is its own slice behind an owner decision\n2030\t(§11, 6c Q2) and is not part of 6c.\n2031\t\n2032\t---\n2033\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6ad7c4d-4e91-4daf-807a-5f71c598506b","uuid":"380048f2-1dc7-489a-98ee-1dcf445e6543","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:16:27.211Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 7). 6a-6c green on three hosts; the row\ncarrier the only writer and reader the semantic-register kernel has; a\ndocumented seam for the holder, checkpoint, ledger and authority paths\n(phase 7 wires it; phase 6 edits none of them); the Stage D/E scenarios\n(`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`) listed with, for\neach, the §5.2 row that exercises its parking shape; the export-recovery\nobject (UCF §7.7.4, format version 1 over the old codec) re-based on the\nrow carrier by phase 7, since it embeds a body; and the free-environment\nretention seam (§6.3) either landed by the 4b track or the explicit\n`:free-env` argument threaded through the holder's export path.\n\n### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n\n| | needs | concurrent with |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6a | 4b stage 1 (branch) | 4b stage 2, 5a-5c, 6c-pre |\n| 6c-pre | nothing of 4b or 5 | everything |\n| 6b | 4b stage 2; 5c (scanners) | 5a, 5b, 6c-pre |\n| 6c | 5a, 5b, 6b, 6c-pre | 5c |\n\nNo file of 4b or phase 5 is edited. The old kernel file\n`debruijn/stack.cljc` is edited by 6c-pre only, for the seam; the\nregister kernel is not edited. `handoff.cljc` and the old harness are\nnot edited. **Amended after 6c (2026-10-13):** 6c proper held to this:\nit created `layout.cljc` and `layout_test.cljc`, extended the 6b harness\nfile and the store-write audit's allowlist, and edited no kernel, no\n`rows.cljc` and no 4b or phase-5 file. The one further kernel edit this\ndesign names, the retained-FFI response-pair seam of both de Bruijn\nkernels (§4.3, 6c-post-D2), is its own slice behind an owner decision\n(§11, 6c Q2) and is not part of 6c.\n\n---\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1999,"totalLines":2621}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KeCZwuTj9fgrHxTVscq9L1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfyz1v5Lhkt7AF2hVWFrR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01T7pq8d1WacqnZDr49vbHfi","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","offset":2493,"limit":130},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1218,"cache_read_input_tokens":160062,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1218},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6ad7c4d-4e91-4daf-807a-5f71c598506b","uuid":"41a59620-152b-45f3-ae16-37c295237061","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:16:28.046Z","request_id":"req_011Cfyz1umyV9qmMipYT6JWh","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01T7pq8d1WacqnZDr49vbHfi":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","offset":2493,"limit":130}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T7pq8d1WacqnZDr49vbHfi","type":"tool_result","content":"2493\t## 11. Owner questions\n2494\t\n2495\tRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\n2496\tand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\n2497\tlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\n2498\tEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\n2499\tdocument.\n2500\t\n2501\t- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n2502\t  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n2503\t  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n2504\t  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n2505\t  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n2506\t  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n2507\t  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n2508\t  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n2509\t  to it?\n2510\t- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n2511\t  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n2512\t  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n2513\t  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n2514\t  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n2515\t  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n2516\t  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n2517\t- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n2518\t  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n2519\t  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n2520\t  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n2521\t  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n2522\t  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n2523\t  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n2524\t  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n2525\t- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n2526\t  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n2527\t  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n2528\t  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n2529\t  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n2530\t  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n2531\t  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n2532\t- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n2533\t  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n2534\t  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n2535\t  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n2536\t  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n2537\t  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n2538\t  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n2539\t  before attachment?\n2540\t- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n2541\t  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n2542\t  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n2543\t  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n2544\t  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n2545\t  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n2546\t  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n2547\t  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n2548\t  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n2549\t  constant-dead code?\n2550\t- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n2551\t  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n2552\t  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n2553\t  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n2554\t  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n2555\t- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n2556\t  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n2557\t  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n2558\t  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n2559\t  entry point?\n2560\t- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n2561\t  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n2562\t  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n2563\t  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n2564\t  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n2565\t- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n2566\t  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n2567\t  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n2568\t\n2569\t**Revision 5 (after 6c) adds the three questions the rulings left\n2570\t(`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`); they are\n2571\tnumbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n2572\t\n2573\t- **6c Q1, the native-lane matrix scope (changes acceptance counts).\n2574\t  ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13.** The de Bruijn lanes run the\n2575\t  shared fragment: law 4 on 61 of the 89 cases, law 5 on 350 of the 534\n2576\t  transfers, tail acceptance on rows 6 and 20 only, because those\n2577\t  kernels die at phase 8 and the reference lane keeps the full matrix.\n2578\t  The owner confirmed the matrix of 61 law-4 cases and 350 law-5\n2579\t  transfers as normative; §5.3 and §8.3 items 10-11 state it so.\n2580\t- **6c Q2, the retained-FFI seam (kernel edit, own slice). Open.** Land\n2581\t  6c-post-D2, the four-line response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels\n2582\t  (§4.3; half a day, dies at phase 8), raising law 5 to 366 and 96 and\n2583\t  emptying `unlowerable`. Recommendation: only if the owner wants row 4\n2584\t  cross-engine before phase 8; otherwise skip.\n2585\t- **6c Q3, closures in the free environment (information; a decision\n2586\t  only if the owner disagrees). Open.** Closures and continuations in\n2587\t  the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (§6.3,\n2588\t  R3) and admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de\n2589\t  Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3\n2590\t  becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n2591\t\n2592\t---\n2593\t\n2594\t## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n2595\t\n2596\t1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n2597\t   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n2598\t   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n2599\t   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n2600\t   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n2601\t   `(get W r)`.\n2602\t2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n2603\t   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n2604\t3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n2605\t   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n2606\t4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n2607\t   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n2608\t   in `layout` likewise.\n2609\t5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n2610\t   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n2611\t6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n2612\t   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n2613\t7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n2614\t   no `#'`.\n2615\t8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n2616\t   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n2617\t9. **Numeric classes (Node).** `pc`, `rd`, `r`, `site` are exact\n2618\t   integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n2619\t   (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n2620\t10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane.\n2621\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6ad7c4d-4e91-4daf-807a-5f71c598506b","uuid":"0245d9f4-2610-47f9-8e17-8e1e0a77a843","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:16:28.058Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7a/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"## 11. Owner questions\n\nRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\nand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\nlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\nEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\ndocument.\n\n- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n  to it?\n- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n  before attachment?\n- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n  constant-dead code?\n- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n  entry point?\n- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n\n**Revision 5 (after 6c) adds the three questions the rulings left\n(`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`); they are\nnumbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n\n- **6c Q1, the native-lane matrix scope (changes acceptance counts).\n  ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13.** The de Bruijn lanes run the\n  shared fragment: law 4 on 61 of the 89 cases, law 5 on 350 of the 534\n  transfers, tail acceptance on rows 6 and 20 only, because those\n  kernels die at phase 8 and the reference lane keeps the full matrix.\n  The owner confirmed the matrix of 61 law-4 cases and 350 law-5\n  transfers as normative; §5.3 and §8.3 items 10-11 state it so.\n- **6c Q2, the retained-FFI seam (kernel edit, own slice). Open.** Land\n  6c-post-D2, the four-line response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels\n  (§4.3; half a day, dies at phase 8), raising law 5 to 366 and 96 and\n  emptying `unlowerable`. Recommendation: only if the owner wants row 4\n  cross-engine before phase 8; otherwise skip.\n- **6c Q3, closures in the free environment (information; a decision\n  only if the owner disagrees). Open.** Closures and continuations in\n  the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (§6.3,\n  R3) and admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de\n  Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3\n  becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n\n1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n   `(get W r)`.\n2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n   in `layout` likewise.\n5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n   no `#'`.\n8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n9. **Numeric classes (Node).** `pc`, `rd`, `r`, `site` are exact\n   integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n   (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane.\n","numLines":129,"startLine":2493,"totalLines":2621}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01T7pq8d1WacqnZDr49vbHfi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
